    "f2d9dc04-eeda-4044-a6ba-2477f1962f4e": {
      "id": "f2d9dc04-eeda-4044-a6ba-2477f1962f4e",
      "message": {
        "id": "f2d9dc04-eeda-4044-a6ba-2477f1962f4e",
        "author": {
          "role": "assistant",
          "name": null,
          "metadata": {}
        },
        "create_time": 1780862764.745501,
        "update_time": null,
        "content": {
          "content_type": "text",
          "parts": [
            "If you're only painting and patching holes, and you've noticed that one exposed section of wall has insulation while the rest doesn't, that's not necessarily unusual.\n\nIn many condo units:\n\n- **Interior walls** between rooms often have no insulation at all.\n- **Some shared walls** between units may have insulation for sound reduction, but not always throughout the entire wall.\n- **Exterior walls** usually contain insulation because they're part of the building envelope.\n- Over 20 years, previous owners, contractors, or building maintenance may have opened and repaired sections differently, resulting in insulation being present only in certain areas.\n\nBefore closing any holes, it may be worth taking photos of:\n- Wall cavities\n- Electrical wiring\n- Plumbing\n- Any insulation present\n- Any signs of water staining or mold\n\nThose photos can be useful later if you ever need to troubleshoot issues or prove the condition of the walls before repairs.\n\nIf the wall that's insulated is adjacent to another condo unit, an elevator shaft, garbage chute, or mechanical room, the insulation may have been installed primarily for noise control rather than thermal insulation.\n\nCan you tell me:\n1. Approximately how old is the condo building?\n2. Is the insulated wall an exterior wall or a wall shared with a neighbor?\n\nThat would help determine whether the missing insulation is typical for your building or something that was altered during a past renovation."
          ]
        },
